00:00:26.403 --> 00:00:28.570
AMY BANZAERT: So today,
we're doing charcoal making.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.990
And we'll talk a bunch about
charcoal making on Friday,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.410
but hopefully, you read the
directions for making it,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.710
so you're at least
a little familiar.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.670
And so one of the things that's
complicated in charcoal making

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.530
is the order of
operations because there

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.520
are steps that you need
to do to make briquettes

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.088
that, if you want to be most
conservative with your energy,

00:00:52.088 --> 00:00:54.630
we need to do while you're doing
the next carbonization burn.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.630
And this is something to
keep in mind if you are

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.230
making charcoal in Nicaragua.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.032
Like if you were
doing a demo, you

00:01:00.032 --> 00:01:01.740
need to have charcoal
to make briquettes,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.110
and you also need to
be able to do a burn.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.700
So you're actually going to burn
ahead of time in order to make

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.380
the briquettes with people as
well as doing the charcoal burn

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.030
because it's a
two-step process--

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.850
one is lighting your
raw material on fire

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:21.840
and making it into
charcoal fines,

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.600
and then the other step
is crushing the charcoal

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.600
and mixing with the binder
and making it into briquettes.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.603
But some of those
processes can be parallel.

00:01:30.603 --> 00:01:32.520
So the first thing that
we actually need to do

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:39.660
is prepare the cassava,
which is our binder which

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.190
is used to keep those
briquettes together.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:43.770
So this is what
cassava looks like.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.750
It's also known
as yuca or manioc.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.910
I think, in Nicaragua,
it's mostly known as yuca.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:48.660
AUDIENCE: Yoo-cah.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.740
AMY BANZAERT: Yuca, thank you.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.310
So this is what you look for.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.280
It's generally not waxed when
you buy it outside the United

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.220
States, but other than that,
it looks exactly like this.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.830
It's sort of like a
sweet potato in that it

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.930
comes much skinnier
and shorter than this

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.240
and it comes much bigger too.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.580
So you can find it in
all sorts of varieties.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.100
It doesn't really
matter what you use.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.900
Bigger diameter it's
nicer because you

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.610
have more meat to skin ratio,
and you have to peel the skin.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.610
Other than that, it doesn't
matter what you get.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:21.270
But you should be able to get it
at any supermarket in Nicaragua

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.510
and smaller markets
sometimes, depending.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:30.300
So if you guys want to
start grating this--

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.780
peeling it and then grating it,
the peels can go in this bag

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.710
and then the grated stuff
can go in this thing.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.520
If we don't have a grater
with you, which you may not,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.580
a spoon works really
well to grate.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.960
It's not as nice as a peeler,
but you can use it to peel.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.990
And [INAUDIBLE] it works
pretty well anywhere.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.192
In terms of graters,
this is probably

00:02:54.192 --> 00:02:55.650
what you're used
to seeing, and you

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.370
want to use the pointy side.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.890
This is one I
bought in Nicaragua.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.415
This is one we bought in Haiti.

00:03:03.415 --> 00:03:05.790
So you can you're going to be
able to find them anywhere.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.800
They're probably not going
to be like OXO brand stuff,

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.720
but you'll be able to find them.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.140
So if some percent of
people wants to get

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.820
started doing that.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:26.010
So the other stuff we're
doing is making the charcoal,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.440
making the charcoal fines.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.310
So you can see that this
oil drum has a hole cut out

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.550
of the top and about
this big is nice so

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:39.930
that there's enough edge
of the top of the drum

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.880
so that when you
put a lid down, you

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.800
don't have to be careful to make
it line up to really cover it

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.570
well, but big enough that you
can load the material in well.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:55.030
On the backside, you
just put in a few holes.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.580
We often put in more
holes than just this many,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:58.720
so I would recommend that.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.550
If I were doing
this drum today, I

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.700
would add one here
and here and here.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.550
The holes are for airflow,
and more holes is better.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.370
On the flip side this is getting
to be pretty big in terms

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.770
of diameter hole because
you don't want your waste

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.990
to all fall out the bottom.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.100
One thing you can do to
manage that, which is in here,

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.370
is you can find this stuff,
which is called chicken wire,

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:24.220
and just throw it in
the bottom to prevent

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:25.600
the waste from falling out.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.470
However, you need to have
some waste that comes out

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.340
these holes at the bottom.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.920
You'll see that we use
fuses to light the drum.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.765
So it's this weird
catch-22, where

00:04:35.765 --> 00:04:37.390
you need it not to
fall out, except you

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:41.380
do so we're not looking to use
this today because the waste

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.590
isn't that fine.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:45.705
The other thing that you can
use chicken wire for-- well,

00:04:45.705 --> 00:04:47.080
there's two other
things, but one

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.080
is, if you're trying to
carbonize waste that's

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.330
pretty small in diameter--
and hopefully you won't be,

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.670
because we don't do it well
yet with the current processes.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:05:02.230
But if you were going to
carbonize rice husks or sawdust

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:04.720
or something like that, the
method we've come up with

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.640
is to take the chicken
wire and roll it

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.480
into cylinders that are
about the same diameter

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.590
as these holes, put
them in the holes,

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:13.780
and the waste goes around
those holes, but not in it

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.100
to help get airflow.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.380
So that's another thing
we use chicken wire for--

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.030
and then a third that
I'll show you in a bit.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.740
So the way you make these holes,
if you have an angle grinder,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:24.520
sweet.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.430
You probably won't,
so you can use

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:30.040
a hammer and a chisel
and sometimes just a nail

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:31.540
to get the hole started.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.350
It's really laborious,
painful to do.

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:37.750
If you can find
someone who you're

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:39.640
working with who's
used to using a chisel

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.182
and used to doing
metal work, they'll

00:05:41.182 --> 00:05:43.710
do a much better
job than you will,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.210
for at least for most of us who
aren't used to using chisels

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.610
on metal most days.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.680
So don't be afraid
to ask for help.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.470
And then when you're
finishing the holes,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.620
down here, you just want
to try to bend them in

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:58.210
as much as possible,
if you can get

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.382
pliers or something
similar to bend it

00:06:00.382 --> 00:06:01.840
so that no one gets
cut when you're

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.190
working with all of this stuff.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:04.960
It's really easy
to get cut and it's

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:06.940
kind of nasty cut in
a dirty environment.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:10.080
So it's nice not to have that.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.580
So what we're going to do now
with that awesome warming that

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.940
makes me really excited is
to load the raw material

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.470
in the drum.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.400
What we're going to try to do is
put layers of the corn stalks,

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.170
which is in the
blue tarp, and then

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:27.160
the corncobs, which is
in the green mesh bag,

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.800
so if you guys want
to start doing that.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.750
Typically, you would take
if you remember to bring one

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.850
on your trip, which you
rarely do in my experience,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.880
you would use one of these to
weigh out how much material you

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.140
were using.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:46.810
In this case, we're not going to
bother with that because we're

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:48.490
just doing a practice
burn and it's cold

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:51.140
and I'm trying to
speed things along.

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:55.090
But generally, for
waste, you're trying

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:59.440
to get about 16 kilograms
into the drum, which is a lot,

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.370
so you have to pack it down.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:05.890
One of my friends likes to
stand on the top of the drum

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:09.640
and sink in when he does it.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.980
But you definitely want
to pack it in pretty well.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.280
So if you guys want to get going
and start loading the drum.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.360
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.080
AMY BANZAERT: One thing
I forgot to mention,

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.950
and it's super easy to forget,
is to put a sniff of any

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:43.150
down the center of the
drum so that the material--

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.510
so there's a chimney in
the center to really help

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.150
the material to light on fire.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:51.630
It's two things to know, number
one is really easy for forget,

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.400
so try really hard not to.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.080
There are instructions
that you'll

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.120
have that go through
each step, and just

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.630
make sure to go through them.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:03.630
The other thing is we've
asked community partners,

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:05.640
do you have a long
stick, and they've

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:09.150
gone out and bought like a
$50 really nice piece of wood

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:13.990
for stuff, when all we need
is any random piece of wood,

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:16.000
metal, you name it will work.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.810
And so just emphasizing
this isn't fancy.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.760
And then as you're
loading, you want

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.310
to pull the stick around and
keep making that chimney.

00:08:54.658 --> 00:08:56.575
AUDIENCE: Don't-- don't
sink in the fire, man.

00:08:56.575 --> 00:08:58.660
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:00.880
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.650
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.230
AMY BANZAERT: We have done
crazy things like that.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:12.625
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.254
AUDIENCE: $0.44.

00:09:21.254 --> 00:09:23.242
[CHUCKLES]

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:30.160
AMY BANZAERT: OK, so now
what you do is get three--

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.900
generally rocks,
but around here,

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.970
it's actually easier to
get bricks than rocks,

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:38.230
so we use bricks on campus.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:42.250
And you want to lift the drum
onto the bricks or rocks.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:46.120
And the thing is,
once this drum is hot

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:50.180
and on fire, we're going to
have to lift it off the bricks,

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.590
and so you want the bricks as
far on the edge as possible

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.350
of the drum to make it really
easy to kick the bricks out

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.580
of the way.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:01.420
So if two people want to
lift the drum onto the bricks

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:03.940
and then optimize
brick placement.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.640
So I would pull that
break out further,

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.500
and I can't see the other one.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:24.480
And then you just pull around
to optimize your chimney,

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:25.840
and then pull this out.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:30.840
So now we're ready to
light it on fire, except--

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.090
so that would be
what I would do back

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.120
before I've done this a million
times and realized my mistake.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.770
So I said we needed to boil some
water on here for the binder,

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:39.270
right?

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:42.990
So we need to get that ready
before we light on fire.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.840
So it's always important before
you actually light the matches

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:49.560
to make sure you're
completely and truly ready,

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.920
both in terms of having
water on the side

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.130
so that if there is some
of uncontrollable fire,

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:59.850
you can put it out quickly,
having all the stuff you need

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.783
to cover up the drum
when you're done,

00:11:01.783 --> 00:11:03.700
having a plan for how
you're going to do that.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:05.658
So we're going to go
through all of that first,

00:11:05.658 --> 00:11:09.330
but one thing we're going to do
is boil water for the binder.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:15.638
So the way we're
going to do that is

00:11:15.638 --> 00:11:16.930
there's going to be two people.

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:19.050
So Kurt, are you going
to help me demonstrate?

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:21.810
Maybe you can just put it down,
maybe a little further away

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:27.240
from the tripod, so we don't--

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:32.250
all right, so we
each take one side.

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:35.220
Sometimes you can wire
it if you're worried,

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.190
and then we lift it up
and then you bring it over

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:42.232
and ideally, you'd have a--

00:11:45.064 --> 00:11:46.740
the one thing I'm
worried about this one

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.080
is that I think the diameter
does not work at all.

00:11:51.712 --> 00:11:52.920
So can we put it on the side?

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:53.730
Can you lift it up?

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:54.550
Lift it up.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:12:00.103
And then if it goes
there, will it stay?

00:12:00.103 --> 00:12:01.020
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:04.150
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, but
it has a curved bottom.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:05.640
So I think we'll go with that.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:08.085
AUDIENCE: Why can't
we sit it inside?

00:12:08.085 --> 00:12:10.710
AMY BANZAERT: Because it's going
to be really hard to take out.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.650
And then do it back.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:20.350
So Kurt, you've just
gotten practice.

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:25.230
so I want two other
people, one from group O

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:31.032
and one from [INAUDIBLE]
to practice it,

00:12:31.032 --> 00:12:33.240
and then you guys will be
the one who actually do it.

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.660
But it's actually worth
practicing before you light it

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:45.900
on fire because if you drop
the cold water, you just

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:49.840
destroy the burn, if you
drop it in the raw material,

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:52.330
but if you drop the boiling
water, you can scald people,

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:57.210
including yourselves,
which is highly unideal.

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:58.320
So it's worth practicing.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:12.780
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:17.270
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, it might
be the worst chosen pot ever,

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:21.012
so hopefully you will have
more success with pot choosing.

00:13:21.012 --> 00:13:22.970
And then you can actually
move this quite a bit

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:25.220
farther away, like to
the edge of the bricks.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:26.765
This drum is going
to be really hot,

00:13:26.765 --> 00:13:28.640
so you won't want to be
near it for too long.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.070
All right, so we're
ready for water boiling.

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:36.200
The other thing I said
we need to be ready for

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.210
is covering up the
drum because you

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.640
want to do that
relatively quickly,

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:44.990
and the process is one
that's easy to misremember

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:47.750
what order to do it
in, especially for me

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:50.720
because we changed the order
a lot as we optimized it.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.200
So first, what you
do is get the lid on,

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.970
which just involves
doing that, and then,

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:06.600
if it didn't quite
get in the right spot,

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.160
you can use this to get
it in the right spot.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:10.920
The other thing you
want to do is notice

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.410
if the lid is kind
of convex or concave.

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:17.430
We're trying to make a seal,
so it's better to have--

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:19.560
I mean, it doesn't matter
a lot, but whichever way

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:20.435
fits a little better.

00:14:20.435 --> 00:14:22.995
So I think that this way
fits a little better, maybe.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:30.480
And then we're going to
take it off the bricks.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:34.980
So for that, what
you do is find--

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.780
you do it at the bricks,
so you lean the drum over,

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.820
and then someone
comes and kicks it.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:44.820
And then you very,
very carefully

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:48.570
lower this drum down because
now it's going a bit at angle,

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:50.220
and then you repeat.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:55.440
There's a shovel around
here that you can also use.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:58.860
Sometimes it's nice to have
two people manipulating

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.250
the drum just for a little
bit of extra safety.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.620
And then once it's all the
way down on the ground,

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:12.480
then we will find something to
cover up this hole, anything

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:14.220
that's around,
and then pour sand

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.620
around the edges on the
top and on the bottom.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.147
And just work as quickly
as possible to get it

00:15:19.147 --> 00:15:21.480
completely sealed so you can't
see any smoke seeping out

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:23.190
anywhere.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:25.251
Are there any questions
about all this?

00:15:25.251 --> 00:15:30.150
All right, so the one
last thing to know

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.570
is, especially when it's
windy, which it often

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.750
is in Nicaragua too, it's a
really pain in the butt to use

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:41.280
matches to light this,
but we're going to,

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:42.840
because it's good
to practice that.

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:44.880
AUDIENCE: Where does
the water come in?

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:45.705
The boiling water?

00:15:45.705 --> 00:15:46.830
AMY BANZAERT: That's first.

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.705
AUDIENCE: We light it
and then boil the water

00:15:48.705 --> 00:15:50.880
and then seal it?

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:53.650
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, but it's
not a ten second process,

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:56.560
so it'll be plenty of time to
get the water when this time.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:59.220
So if five people want to
all grab matches and try

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:02.797
to start lighting it,
hopefully we'll have success.

00:16:02.797 --> 00:16:05.130
So did you guys all-- everyone
with a match should know,

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:08.220
you're lighting the
bottom at the fuses.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.365
OK, so you guys can start.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:24.380
AUDIENCE: I don't think--

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:25.963
I think it's going
to [INAUDIBLE] now.

00:16:25.963 --> 00:16:26.470
[INAUDIBLE]

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:28.940
AMY BANZAERT: Exactly, thanks.

00:16:31.396 --> 00:16:32.396
AUDIENCE: Wait, hold on.

00:16:32.396 --> 00:16:33.390
Are we recording this?

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.610
AMY BANZAERT: No, so
what's happening--

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.422
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:37.422 --> 00:16:38.328
[CLANG]

00:16:38.328 --> 00:16:39.687
AUDIENCE: That's awesome.

00:16:39.687 --> 00:16:41.160
AMY BANZAERT: So
right now, you're

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.400
seeing this incredibly nasty
disgusting smoke, right?

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:48.210
And so this is all the
volatiles in the raw material

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:50.370
that we don't want to be
breathing in when you're

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:52.770
cooking inside, burning off.

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:56.580
So you can see there actually
isn't any fire up at the top

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:58.860
when the wind blows it.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:02.190
The material hasn't actually
lit on fire completely,

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:06.119
so it might not be a bad
idea, if we can do it,

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:07.619
to light some matches
and throw them

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:10.079
in the top to try to
get the topside on fire.

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:17.700
We're not going to be boiling
the water until the smoke blows

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.099
away.

00:17:20.099 --> 00:17:25.140
At some point, the smoke
will transition to clear.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:29.975
And when that happens, that's
when we'll do the water.

00:17:29.975 --> 00:17:31.350
And this is smoke
that you really

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:34.020
don't want to be
breathing in, if it's not

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:35.040
obvious by the color.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:44.970
Yeah, if it's too hard,
just don't worry about it

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:47.630
because it's not worth--

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:49.130
AUDIENCE: It looks
like an airplane.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:53.336
AUDIENCE: Oh, clever.

00:17:53.336 --> 00:17:54.288
AUDIENCE: Shit.

00:17:58.637 --> 00:18:00.470
AMY BANZAERT: Oh, so
the other thing to know

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:06.260
is that by Cambridge,
Mass law, if we're

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:08.570
going to be lighting a
fire, we have to be cooking.

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:11.510
So there are marshmallows
and English muffins.

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:15.920
Once this smoke clears,
feel free to roast

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:19.415
a marshmallow on whatever
you can find or toast

00:18:19.415 --> 00:18:21.470
an English muffin.

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:24.540
And then there's Nutella
to put on English muffins.

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:26.730
So at this point, we can
probably do the water.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:34.160
So what did you say?

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:37.220
Yeah, it's nice to
use the waste heat.

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:49.820
Nice.

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:54.620
The question of how long to
wait until you cover the drum

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:55.730
is always tricky.

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:57.560
At this point, it's
probably a good time

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:59.778
to cover it which the
water isn't boiled,

00:18:59.778 --> 00:19:01.320
but we're just going
to go with that.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:03.070
So if you guys want
to take the water off.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:07.730
And then who wants
to throw the lid on?

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:27.125
So can someone grab the shovel?

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:36.230
So the lid is getting
caught into the hole.

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:38.337
And now, who's going
to kick the brick?

00:19:38.337 --> 00:19:39.620
AUDIENCE: I will.

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:40.328
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:19:47.902 --> 00:19:49.360
And who wants to
do the next brick?

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:11.150
OK, can someone grab like an
English muffin or whatever

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:12.410
to put in that center hole?

00:20:22.582 --> 00:20:23.790
English muffin is way cooler.

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.123
We can claim that we're cooking.

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.810
And then there's a
whole other bag of sand,

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:44.618
if you guys want to
help get this sealed.

00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:51.605
AUDIENCE: And we can
get even closer now.

00:20:51.605 --> 00:20:55.260
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I thought
it's warmer after the fire.

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:57.760
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:02.562
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:15.910
AMY BANZAERT: And you can
obviously use dirt for this.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.625
You don't have to use special
sand that you acquire.

00:21:22.552 --> 00:21:24.010
AUDIENCE: So what
was the rationale

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:27.065
for calling [INAUDIBLE]?

00:21:27.065 --> 00:21:28.690
AMY BANZAERT: For
when I made the call?

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:33.610
So the flames-- so it's
sort of a time thing

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:36.460
and sort of an experience
thing, but it's

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:38.680
looking at how the quality
of the flame and the flame

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:43.090
started getting really strong,
which means everything is fully

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:45.460
on fire, and if you don't
cover it up quickly,

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:47.460
you're just going to burn
out all your material.

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:54.850
No, so this is all smoke.

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:57.310
This lid is actually
really hard to seal.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:08.510
In terms of getting
a lid, oftentimes you

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:13.880
can just buy flat pot lids
and that works pretty well.

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:16.820
So when you're buying a
pot, if you need a pot,

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:19.350
if your community partner
needs you to buy a pot,

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:21.110
you can also be
buying a pot lid that

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:24.490
goes over your drum,
this big diameter.

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:29.710
I think that's water,
actually, going off.

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:31.930
We try to seal as
much as possible,

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:36.430
but it's impossible
to do it perfectly.

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:41.050
So that's that, and now
you just wait two hours

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:46.450
and then you come back and the
material should be carbonized.

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:50.740
And if you are impatient and
you open it after an hour,

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:52.173
it'll probably
catch back on fire,

00:22:52.173 --> 00:22:54.590
so then you have to just cover
it up again really quickly.

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:56.890
So it's currently
almost 1:00, so that

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:59.830
means we'll be able to
wait almost two hours

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:03.970
and then open it up before a
class is over, which is great.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:08.470
So next, what I want to do
is light these stoves on fire

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:13.410
so we can make hot chocolate,
and then we'll make briquettes.

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:15.160
When you're making
just a regular charcoal

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:18.280
fire in a developing
country, you can typically

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:20.830
get a special type of wood--

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:22.612
I forget what it's
called in Nicaragua.

00:23:22.612 --> 00:23:23.570
I'll try to look it up.

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:24.310
Do you do know?

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:25.310
Is it ocote?

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:26.320
That's what I was
remembering this morning,

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:28.612
but then I was like, oh, I
think that's something else.

00:23:28.612 --> 00:23:31.240
OK, so it's called ocote,
and it's basically,

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:35.260
from my understanding, is just
a pine that's really sappy

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:38.920
and this is the equivalent
of that in the US.

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:41.500
And so you light
those on fire and they

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:44.560
help the briquettes, the
charcoal, catch on fire.

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:47.140
This is wood charcoal that I
just bought from Whole Foods

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:49.750
because our charcoal
is so labor intensive

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:53.660
that it pains me to use it
up for something like this.

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:57.160
So if you guys want to
try to those on fire now.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:00.542
AUDIENCE: So just
like light it like--

00:24:00.542 --> 00:24:02.500
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so
those three brown things

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.530
are what you're
trying to get lit.

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:08.530
So these are two
different stoves

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:10.400
that we'll be talking
about on Friday.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:16.210
So the one that Sid and
Misty are lighting right now

00:24:16.210 --> 00:24:20.110
is just a really simple
relatively simple charcoal

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:20.980
stove.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:24.490
I forget what country it's
from, but you can find them

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:27.310
all over the place.

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:33.670
And so it has very little
insulation, obviously.

00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:36.280
The one that Kurt
and is that Juan?

00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:37.000
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:41.440
AMY BANZAERT: --are lighting
is called a gecko stove,

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:44.932
and we'll talk about
it a bit, but it's

00:24:44.932 --> 00:24:46.390
a well-designed
stove that actually

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:47.830
has some decent insulation.

00:25:00.598 --> 00:25:02.765
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] what
would be used as a match.

00:25:02.765 --> 00:25:03.350
Maybe like--

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:04.858
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:04.858 --> 00:25:07.400
AMY BANZAERT: It's just that
the way they're designed to work

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.020
is like this on top.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.273
AUDIENCE: It's just
like getting black.

00:25:14.273 --> 00:25:15.440
AUDIENCE: It's getting gray.

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:17.224
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:20.690
But it's like cold.

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:23.231
I just put it on.

00:25:23.231 --> 00:25:26.710
AUDIENCE: It doesn't
have much air.

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:27.595
Yeah.

00:25:27.595 --> 00:25:28.720
AUDIENCE: Why is it yellow?

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:30.053
AUDIENCE: It smells really good.

00:25:30.053 --> 00:25:33.058
AUDIENCE: That's how
they always burn.

00:25:33.058 --> 00:25:36.477
AUDIENCE: It's definitely, like,
very marshmallowy in the rest

00:25:36.477 --> 00:25:39.470
of it, though.

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:43.950
AMY BANZAERT: So after
the carbonization is done,

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:48.440
the next step is to take all
the charcoal fines out and crush

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:49.320
them.

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:53.300
And so this is actually
mostly crushed,

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:55.770
and it's this mishmash of stuff.

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.840
But there's some good
examples in here.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:01.910
So you'll see some stuff
that's just dark brown but not

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:05.300
actually black, and this you
should toss because it's not

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:08.720
fully carbonized enough, and
so it'll make really bad, smoky

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:10.040
briquettes.

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:17.210
Similar-- like this corncob
is not fully carbonized.

00:26:17.210 --> 00:26:21.290
With regular corncobs, one thing
to check if they look black

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:22.790
on the outside just
break them apart

00:26:22.790 --> 00:26:25.820
and make sure they're
black on the inside.

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:28.697
When you look in
the drum, you're

00:26:28.697 --> 00:26:29.780
going to see three things.

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:32.210
You're going to see,
hopefully, majority

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:35.480
is black material like this,
except not in the crushed form

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:37.940
that you see down
below; some that'll

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:40.520
be that brown stuff
that's undercarbonized;

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:45.020
and some that'll
be gray and ashy.

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:46.250
That's over carbonized.

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:48.500
And so if you have
a ton of gray ash

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:50.877
that means you had let
the fire go too long

00:26:50.877 --> 00:26:52.460
you should have
covered it up earlier.

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:55.820
If you have a ton that's
on the brown side,

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:57.380
you didn't let it go far enough.

00:26:57.380 --> 00:27:03.080
If you have mostly black
with a little bit of each,

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.210
that's a good indicator
that you had a good burn.

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:08.570
You're never going to
get perfect 100% all even

00:27:08.570 --> 00:27:12.260
because this is not
exactly a high tech kiln,

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:15.965
and so you're not going to
get even temperature flow

00:27:15.965 --> 00:27:17.090
throughout the whole thing.

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:24.163
Some of the things on the
form that you fill out

00:27:24.163 --> 00:27:25.580
when you do a
charcoal burn, which

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:26.955
I haven't been
doing because it's

00:27:26.955 --> 00:27:29.900
too hard to talk and
remember to fill this out,

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:32.640
are things like air
temperature and humidity,

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:35.255
which you probably won't
have a sensor for that,

00:27:35.255 --> 00:27:37.130
but if you happen to
know, you can record it.

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:39.350
And otherwise, by
recording the location,

00:27:39.350 --> 00:27:41.430
we can look it up afterwards.

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:44.683
So just fill all these things
out as well as you can,

00:27:44.683 --> 00:27:46.100
but there are
probably going to be

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:48.800
some things you can't recall.

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:51.410
But especially if you
bring any charcoal back

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:53.660
for testing in the lab,
it's really helpful

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:54.900
to have filled these out.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:58.550
And then there's one
side that's the form,

00:27:58.550 --> 00:28:02.420
and then the other side has the
directions, both what you need

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:03.500
and then what to do.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:10.370
So hopefully that's enough to
get you successfully making

00:28:10.370 --> 00:28:10.940
charcoal.

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:15.260
So anyway, to crush the
briquettes or the fines

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:18.260
into powder, what
we typically do

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:22.220
is load it into two rice bags.

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:23.840
And rice bags are
pretty ubiquitous.

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:25.970
You can get them
almost anywhere.

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:28.310
And then you just
jump on the bags.

00:28:28.310 --> 00:28:32.270
When you're done,
you get a mixture

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:34.370
that kind of looks
like this, where

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:40.100
you have a lot of stuff
that's really well crushed--

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:42.470
hopefully you have a lot of
that-- and then some stuff

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:43.950
that was poorly crushed.

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:45.950
And so what you
need to do is grab

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:49.610
the stuff that was poorly
crushed and crush it.

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:54.470
The super crushed fine
stuff acts as a sponge

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:58.070
to dampen the act of
crushing everything else,

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.390
so it's pretty annoying.

00:28:59.390 --> 00:29:03.650
So there is not enough
ag waste in the world

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:07.290
to solve the needs of
fuel usage in the world.

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:11.840
However, there is enough to have
it be one part of the solution,

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:14.370
and then different
areas will have

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:15.620
different amounts of ag waste.

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:17.870
So places that have no
farming, obviously, you're

00:29:17.870 --> 00:29:20.120
not going to have any ag
waste and it's inappropriate.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:21.912
Places where there are
farming, some people

00:29:21.912 --> 00:29:24.500
use their ag waste either
to feed their cattle

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:29.120
or to replenish the
soil or to light

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:31.280
the fires to process the
sugar juice that they

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:32.690
dispose of the sugar cane.

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:35.520
In all those areas, you
can't use this process.

00:29:35.520 --> 00:29:38.090
But there are many areas
where the ag waste is just

00:29:38.090 --> 00:29:39.545
sitting in rotting.

00:29:39.545 --> 00:29:40.920
And I'll get into
this on Friday,

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:44.090
but that makes methane gas,
which is a worse greenhouse

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:46.400
gas than the stuff
we're emitting

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:50.570
by doing that nasty
process in the oil drum.

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:53.120
So that we're doing the
environmental credit

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:58.315
by not letting it rot and
making something that is useful.

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:10.500
All right, that's not
boiling, but it's pretty hot,

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:14.080
so we're going to go with
it and see what happens.

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:20.570
So you can see on the
sheet the directions.

00:30:20.570 --> 00:30:23.980
There's a ratio
for how much binder

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:30.340
or how much cassava to how
much water to how many--

00:30:32.950 --> 00:30:36.822
to an oil drums
worth of charcoal.

00:30:36.822 --> 00:30:39.280
And there's a bunch of different
methods for using cassava.

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:43.100
So the easiest is just
grating it and dumping it.

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:46.160
But that means you're using
up all the food to cook with.

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:51.160
So another option is to squeeze
out the grated material,

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:52.960
and you can do that
with a cheesecloth.

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:55.480
But it's pretty
juicy, so you can

00:30:55.480 --> 00:31:03.490
see you do get some liquid
out, but it's not a great rate

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:04.870
because a lot of it is the meat.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:07.480
But anyway, so you can
take those squeezings

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:10.510
and just use that
as your binder,

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:14.110
and then save the
meat to cook with.

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:18.190
And you can make a really great
cassava cake with the meat,

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:20.030
among other things.

00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:23.110
So in terms of the
ratio, the measurements

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:27.910
are done in milliliters, which
is a little ridiculous when

00:31:27.910 --> 00:31:28.970
you're in the field.

00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:30.760
And so the other
rule of thumb is

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:36.040
that a yuca about the
size that I showed you

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:39.007
is right for one oil
drum, fully grated.

00:31:39.007 --> 00:31:40.840
But if you're doing the
squeeze method then,

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:43.652
you need to use more
cassava because you're

00:31:43.652 --> 00:31:44.860
getting a whole lot less out.

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:52.650
And then, you put
the cassava in,

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:55.620
and you want to do it while it's
boiling because the starch goes

00:31:55.620 --> 00:32:00.150
through a transition.

00:32:00.150 --> 00:32:02.370
It's much like making candy.

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:04.530
So you get your
sugar hot enough,

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:06.660
and then it gets
into a caramel state.

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:10.860
So we're getting to the
caramel state of yuca.

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:13.440
It can get kind of clumpy,
so you want to stir it,

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:18.360
and then once it's been
boiling for a bit and stirring,

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:20.850
you take it off, and
hopefully, what you

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:25.480
have is snot-like consistency.

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:29.460
Yuca is what you
get tapioca from,

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:33.090
so we're basically making
a very unprocessed version

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:34.710
of tapioca pudding.

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:36.120
It doesn't taste nearly as good.

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:45.150
So now we need to mix
this with the charcoal.

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:48.900
So there's a five gallon bucket
that's gray on the table.

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:49.845
If you can grab that.

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:57.090
So you can see, most of this
charcoal is well-crushed.

00:32:57.090 --> 00:33:04.630
The diameter of charcoal that
you're going for is bigger--

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:07.710
this is a snot-like is one
of those classic measurements

00:33:07.710 --> 00:33:09.720
that everyone in the
world can understand.

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:11.483
This is one that you
can only understand

00:33:11.483 --> 00:33:12.900
if you go to Trader
Joe's, so it's

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:15.660
on the opposite end of
the useful spectrum,

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:17.460
but wasabi covered pea.

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:25.020
So I'm eyeballing
all these ratios

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:26.730
because I've been
doing this for a while,

00:33:26.730 --> 00:33:29.040
but this is the kind
of thing that you

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:32.580
do want to actually
follow directions.

00:33:32.580 --> 00:33:36.450
If there's not enough binder,
it'll be pretty obvious--

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:42.460
a little more-- because
it won't stick together.

00:33:42.460 --> 00:33:46.320
But if there's too much, it
makes smoky briquettes, again,

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:50.250
because it's
uncarbonized material.

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:53.580
And then it's really important,
before you start briquetting,

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:57.270
to make sure you mix
everything, like making a cake,

00:33:57.270 --> 00:34:00.090
where if you have a bunch of
flour left over at the end,

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:01.170
that stinks.

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:03.960
So you don't want any
charcoal powder left over.

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:08.250
And another thing to notice
is that the binder now

00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:09.179
is really hot.

00:34:09.179 --> 00:34:10.980
I think we're probably good.

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:14.563
And so if you mix
this with your hand--

00:34:14.563 --> 00:34:16.980
and it's actually easier to
mix by hand than with anything

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:17.520
else--

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:20.580
you'll burn your hand,
and it's like hot glue

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:23.620
in that once it's on, it's
really hard to get off.

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:29.010
So those are my tips to you
for making this more pleasant.

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:32.438
So we have a few
different designs

00:34:32.438 --> 00:34:33.480
of our [INAUDIBLE] press.

00:34:33.480 --> 00:34:35.159
So I'll talk about
this more on Friday,

00:34:35.159 --> 00:34:38.550
but this is a really simple nice
device that you can basically

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:40.170
make anywhere for about $2.

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:46.469
So the way it works is it's
three pieces, a bottom pusher

00:34:46.469 --> 00:34:50.280
piece, a cup, and
then a top plunger.

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:57.390
So you load the
material into the cup,

00:34:57.390 --> 00:34:59.700
and you can just do that
directly with your hand.

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:05.130
You can put it in anything where
this part can rest on something

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:06.900
and the center part
doesn't get pushed up.

00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:09.690
So we have these wood
blocks, but you can usually

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:14.773
find a cement brick will work,
or put two pieces of table

00:35:14.773 --> 00:35:15.690
together, or whatever.

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:17.640
There's lots of options.

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:21.360
And then can I have a hammer?

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:27.030
And then you put the plunger on,
you give it a few good whacks,

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:29.670
and then you just pop
this out on the side

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:33.150
to make your briquette.

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:35.590
The charcoal will
build up right here,

00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:38.455
and so every so often,
you have to clean it off.

00:35:38.455 --> 00:35:40.080
You'll also notice
as you're doing this

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:42.910
that a lot of
charcoal gets wasted.

00:35:42.910 --> 00:35:45.180
And so it's smart
to actually do this

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.880
on some of tarp or
rice bag or something

00:35:47.880 --> 00:35:49.245
so you can capture the charcoal.

00:35:49.245 --> 00:35:51.120
And you usually waste
enough charcoal to make

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:54.360
a few briquettes, and it's
painful to waste that charcoal.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:56.490
So once the briquettes
are made, they

00:35:56.490 --> 00:35:59.640
need to dry in the
sun for a few days,

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:01.600
and then you can cook with them.

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:04.530
However, if you want them to
be really sturdy so that they

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:08.470
can handle being
transported to market,

00:36:08.470 --> 00:36:11.070
they can kind of get crumbly.

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:17.580
And so what you can do is
the next use for chicken wire

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:20.640
is to make a basket like this
that holds your briquettes.

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:23.110
And then right before
you put the lid on,

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:25.140
you can throw this
into the drum.

00:36:25.140 --> 00:36:28.570
And it's totally ridiculous,
but you managed to do it

00:36:28.570 --> 00:36:31.950
and it works so that these
don't actually light on fire,

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:35.520
they just get hot enough to--

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:37.590
well, not just to
dry, because you do

00:36:37.590 --> 00:36:39.750
have to dry them
in the sun first,

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:43.200
but then they get hot
enough that the starch goes

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:45.328
through yet another
phase transition.

00:36:45.328 --> 00:36:47.120
So when you're thinking
about making candy,

00:36:47.120 --> 00:36:51.372
it goes from the caramel
stage to the hard candy stage.

00:36:51.372 --> 00:36:52.830
So you get to the
hard candy stage,

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:54.770
and then you have
hard briquettes.

00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:57.500
So if that's important to
whoever is making them--

00:36:57.500 --> 00:36:59.490
if you're making them
for use at your house,

00:36:59.490 --> 00:37:00.710
it doesn't matter at all.

00:37:00.710 --> 00:37:03.080
If you're making
them for use to sell,

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:05.330
then you probably
want to do this stage.

00:37:05.330 --> 00:37:07.310
And you can make a
bunch of these bags

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:12.110
and just throw them in, but
I just have one for show.

00:37:12.110 --> 00:37:14.390
So if people want
to help, though,

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:17.060
but before we take off this
brick, we should get as much

00:37:17.060 --> 00:37:19.520
of the sand off as possible.

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:24.650
There's actually this,
which helps a lot,

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:26.030
but you rarely
have in the field.

00:37:32.132 --> 00:37:33.270
Yeah, you can do that.

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:48.340
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
I was, like,

00:37:48.340 --> 00:37:50.540
hugging it for the whole hour.

00:37:55.030 --> 00:38:00.040
AMY BANZAERT: So you can see
that it's pretty well black,

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:02.990
and when you break it apart,
it's black on the inside.

00:38:02.990 --> 00:38:07.706
So it's kind of nasty.

00:38:07.706 --> 00:38:09.760
AUDIENCE: It's so
nasty, like burny.

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:10.870
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:10.870 --> 00:38:11.703
AMY BANZAERT: Burny?

00:38:11.703 --> 00:38:12.400
Yeah.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:15.580
So good job.

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:18.520
When you are getting this
out, what usually works well

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:22.420
is to dump it on its side, being
kind of careful that not too

00:38:22.420 --> 00:38:23.770
much falls out of the bottom.

00:38:23.770 --> 00:38:31.480
And then you can tip it and
empty it into your rice bag,

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:33.820
and then, oftentimes,
you have to reach in

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:35.350
to scoop out the
last bits, which

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:37.230
is a little unpleasant
but not too bad.

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:41.740
So actually, yeah, it would
be good to do that now.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:43.030
Yeah, it would.

00:38:43.030 --> 00:38:46.900
So we need to get the
rest of the sand off,

00:38:46.900 --> 00:38:48.250
and then we can do that.

00:38:51.450 --> 00:38:52.530
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:55.150
AMY BANZAERT: It's easier
if you just get it all off.

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:02.900
All right, so
that's pretty good.

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:05.950
We're not we're not looking
for zero particles of sand,

00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:09.153
just not too, too many.

00:39:09.153 --> 00:39:10.070
AUDIENCE: So that way?

00:39:10.070 --> 00:39:11.018
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:39:11.018 --> 00:39:11.966
[INAUDIBLE]

00:39:17.180 --> 00:39:18.790
So we should tilt it over?

00:39:18.790 --> 00:39:20.318
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:39:20.318 --> 00:39:21.150
AUDIENCE: Wow.

00:39:21.150 --> 00:39:22.942
AMY BANZAERT: Do you
have it over the hole?

00:39:38.630 --> 00:39:39.500
It's still hot.

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:41.650
[CLANG]

